WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.760
How does a stomach know a meal is coming?

2
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.960
Can it start work before any food arrives?

3
00:00:10.083 --> 00:00:15.063
The first film showed the gut as a one-way line, with several stretches working at

4
00:00:15.063 --> 00:00:19.163
once. Some signals ready a stretch before food arrives.

5
00:00:20.543 --> 00:00:22.063
Others answer once it is there.

6
00:00:23.083 --> 00:00:27.483
Ivan Pavlov's laboratory, in Saint Petersburg, asked this of dogs.

7
00:00:28.783 --> 00:00:32.223
The dogs had recovered from surgery, and were awake and eating.

8
00:00:33.463 --> 00:00:37.323
So one gland at a time could be studied in a whole, living animal.

9
00:00:38.433 --> 00:00:41.673
In one dog, the esophagus was opened at the neck.

10
00:00:43.013 --> 00:00:45.113
Food the dog swallowed fell out into a dish.

11
00:00:46.273 --> 00:00:47.653
It never reached the stomach.

12
00:00:48.683 --> 00:00:53.423
A second opening, called a fistula, let the workers collect juice straight from the

13
00:00:53.423 --> 00:00:54.643
stomach into a flask.

14
00:00:55.717 --> 00:00:59.357
Suppose stomach juice flows only when food touches the stomach.

15
00:01:00.637 --> 00:01:02.717
What should the flask collect while this dog eats?

16
00:01:09.183 --> 00:01:12.123
After a delay of some minutes, acid juice flowed.

17
00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:15.463
It came from the stomach, through the fistula.

18
00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:19.583
The dog was eating, but no food had reached its stomach.

19
00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:22.687
Then both vagus nerves were cut.

20
00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:26.607
These nerves run from the brainstem to the stomach.

21
00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:30.327
Now the dog ate, and the flask stayed empty.

22
00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.230
So in the dog, nerves start the stomach early.

23
00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:40.330
This is the cephalic phase, set off by the sight, smell, taste and chewing of food.

24
00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:43.467
This rules in a nerve-driven start.

25
00:01:44.667 --> 00:01:48.367
It does not rule out later steps, once food is inside the gut.

26
00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:51.090
Here are two later steps.

27
00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:57.570
Chyme, the stomach's acid soup of food, moves on into the duodenum, the first part

28
00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:58.710
of the small intestine.

29
00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:01.350
These steps use hormones.

30
00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:07.210
A hormone is a substance released into the blood, which acts on distant target

31
00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:11.150
cells. The blood carries the message to the next gland.

32
00:02:12.233 --> 00:02:17.873
Acid arriving there makes the duodenal lining release a hormone, secretin, into the

33
00:02:17.873 --> 00:02:23.953
blood. The pancreas answers with fluid rich in bicarbonate, which neutralizes the

34
00:02:23.953 --> 00:02:24.953
acid.

35
00:02:25.417 --> 00:02:31.217
Fat and protein arriving release a second hormone, cholecystokinin, called CCK.

36
00:02:32.437 --> 00:02:36.397
The pancreas sends enzymes, and the gallbladder squeezes out bile.

37
00:02:37.417 --> 00:02:40.097
So nerves start the stomach before food arrives.

38
00:02:41.217 --> 00:02:45.157
And a stretch that food has just reached signals the glands that serve it.

39
00:02:46.297 --> 00:02:50.737
The duodenum reports acid, fat and protein, and the right glands answer.
